  }
  .footer__links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 20px;
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  .footer__links a {
    color: var(--plasma);
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }
  .footer__links a:hover {
    color: var(--bone);
  }
  .footer__legal {
    font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--bone-faint);
  }
  .footer__legal a {
    color: var(--bone-faint);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--iron-3);
  }
  .footer__legal a:hover {
    color: var(--ember);
    border-color: var(--ember);
  }
    .btn {
      min-height: 44px;
    }
    .footer__inner {
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 32px;
    }
    .footer__meta {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: flex-end;
      gap: 26px;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
    }
  /* ================= UNDER 900px: THE STORY, STACKED =================
     The stage never goes live here (the canvas is display:none under 900px, and it also
     stays dark on reduced motion and without WebGL2), so the runway never grows and every
     beat below the landing sat display:none or visibility:hidden. The page was a hero and
     a footer.

     This block tells the SAME story in the SAME order rather than inventing a phone
     version: landing, the four stage beats with their stills, what it costs, the tutorial
     series, then the doors again. Nothing above this line changes, so the desktop
     cinematic is untouched; every rule here is scoped to the fallback.

     Desktop's positioning is absolute against a pinned one-screen hero. Mobile undoes
     exactly that: static flow, full column width, normal document order. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------- shared -----
       Type sized for a narrow column, and the centred alignment Adrian ruled on
       2026-08-20. BOTH paths get these: the cinematic and the fallback say the same
       words at the same size, they only stage them differently. */
    .ast-hero__tagline { font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 9vw, 3rem); }
    .ast-hero__line { font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 4.4vw, 1.3rem); }
    .ast-beat__head { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 7.6vw, 2.6rem); }
    .ast-beat__line { font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 4.2vw, 1.25rem); }
    .ast-price__lede { font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 4.2vw, 1.25rem); }
    .ast-price__note,
    .ast-price__contact { font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 3.6vw, 1.05rem); }
    .ast-reels__title { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 7.6vw, 2.6rem); }

    /* CENTRE EVERYTHING (Adrian, 2026-08-20), the same call he made for the homepage.
       The desktop composition is a LEFT FLANK with the character holding the rest of the
       frame; at this width there is no flank, so the column is the whole screen and a
       centred stack is what the page becomes. */
    .ast-hero__intro,
    .ast-hero__actions,
    .ast-beat,
    .ast-price,
    .ast-end,
    .ast-reels__head { text-align: center; }
    /* text-align cannot reach the mark: the lockup is a COLUMN flex, so its horizontal
       axis is the cross axis and align-items: flex-start from the base rule is what
       holds it left. justify-content would be the wrong axis here. */
    .ast-hero__lockup { align-items: center; }
    /* Same reason. These door rows are flex, so the centring has to be told to them
       rather than inherited as text. */
    .ast-hero__actions :global(.getblock__actions),
    .ast-price :global(.getblock__actions),
    .ast-end :global(.getblock__actions) {
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
    }
    /* The glyph is centred on the card, which works while the strip rides by at a third
       of the width. At full column width it lands squarely on the thumbnail's own
       burned-in title, so give it a corner and a disc to sit on. */
    .ast-reel__play {
      inset: auto 0.75rem 0.75rem auto;
      display: grid;
      place-items: center;
      width: 3rem;
      height: 3rem;
      padding-left: 0.2rem;
      font-size: 1.15rem;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: rgba(11, 14, 16, 0.72);
      border: 1px solid var(--iron-2);
      opacity: 1;
    }

    /* -------------------------------------------------- THE CINEMATIC, ON A PHONE ----
       Adrian, 2026-08-20: "I'm missing the whole story of building the character." The
       build IS this page. It lives in the runway (fitting room, the skeleton growing, the
       skin solidifying, alive) and a flowed column of screenshots is not that story, it
       is a summary of it. So on this path the runway and the sticky stage STAY, and the
       copy is recomposed around a centred character.

       Everything here is scoped to [data-live]. The fallback below keeps the flowed
       column, because with no stage running there is no story to ride. */
    /* THE LANDING IS A REAL FLOW AT THIS WIDTH, not three absolute anchors. Stanza, then
       the trailer, then the doors, distributed by the box rather than by percentages.
       Adrian ruled the trailer "right in the middle" here (2026-08-20), and dead centre is
       what it was: top 50% with a translate. That overlapped the stanza by 73px at 320, 46
       at 360 and 8 at 390, because the stanza's height is set by how the copy wraps and no
       fixed anchor can know that. Measured, not guessed at.
       There is no fixed number left to drift: space-between divides whatever slack the
       screen has. Every other child of the hero is absolutely positioned (canvas, scrim,
       art, beats, reels, price, close), so none of them joins the flex box. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-hero {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      justify-content: space-between;
      padding: calc(var(--header-h) + 3svh) 1.25rem 7svh;
    }
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-hero__intro,
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-hero__media,
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-hero__actions {
      position: static;
      left: auto;
      right: auto;
      top: auto;
      bottom: auto;
      max-width: none;
      width: auto;
      /* The landing-fade travel, restored by hand: desktop replaces it with a
         translateX(-50%) to centre the doors, and centring is the flex box's job here. */
      transform: translateY(calc(var(--aland, 0) * -18px));
    }

    /* COPY AT THE TOP, SCREENSHOT ON THE FLOOR, HER IN BETWEEN (Adrian, 2026-08-20: the
       image "is blocking the cool animation we have going on with the girl"). The block used
       to be a single run of copy-then-image starting below the header, which put the
       screenshot across her upper body at 242 to 439 of 844: the middle of the frame, which
       is exactly where she is.

       So the beat spans the whole screen and splits: the copy sits at the top, the
       screenshot is pushed to the bottom by an auto margin, and the band between them is
       hers. No fixed offsets, so a beat whose title wraps to two lines takes the room from
       the gap rather than from the art. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-beats {
      left: 0;
      right: 0;
      top: calc(var(--header-h) + 1.5svh);
      bottom: 4svh;
      width: auto;
      padding: 0 1.25rem;
    }
    /* Fills the crossfade cell rather than sitting at its top, so there is a full screen for
       the split to divide. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-beat {
      align-self: stretch;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    /* THE SCREENSHOTS COME BACK HERE (Adrian, 2026-08-20: "those images are not showing up
       on mobile"). I had hidden them during the port, reasoning they would print over the
       character. They do not: the copy holds the top band and the art stops well short of
       halfway, so the character still owns the bottom of the frame. That was my call to
       make and it was the wrong one; the screenshots are the beat's evidence.

       The clip on the fourth beat stays hidden, and that one is not a composition choice:
       it is 1.6 MB and it is scrubbed by a loop that deliberately never loads it at this
       width. Showing it here would mean paying for it on a phone. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-beats :global(.ast-beat__img) {
      width: 100%;
      /* auto, not a fixed gap: this is what pins it to the floor of the beat. */
      margin-top: auto;
    }
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-beats .ast-beat__vid { display: none; }

    /* The reels keep their ride; only the lane widens to the column. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-reels {
      left: 0;
      right: 0;
      width: auto;
      padding: 0 1.25rem;
    }
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-reels__head {
      left: 0;
      right: 0;
      top: calc(var(--header-h) + 2svh);
      width: auto;
      padding: 0 1.25rem;
    }
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-price,
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-end {
      left: 0;
      right: 0;
      padding: 0 1.25rem;
    }
    /* THE CLOSING SCREEN FRAMES THE CENTRE (Adrian, 2026-08-21). Title and price at the top,
       doors on the floor, and the standalone-app caveat riding just above them, so the middle
       of the screen is left to her instead of the copy running down it in one block. Desktop
       already read this way and is left alone.

       .ast-price takes a bottom as well as a top, which turns it into a box with slack in it,
       and the caveat's auto margin drops to the floor of that box. Nothing is pinned to a
       fixed offset, so a price line that wraps to an extra row on a narrow phone takes the
       room out of the gap rather than shoving the caveat into the doors. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-price {
      top: calc(var(--header-h) + 3svh);
      bottom: 25svh;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
    }
    /* Child selector, not a bare class: .ast-price__note sets its own margin-top further down
       the sheet, and at equal specificity the later rule wins. This has to outrank it. */
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-price > .ast-price__note { margin-top: auto; }
    .ast-scroll[data-live] .ast-end { top: auto; bottom: 5svh; }

    /* --------------------------------------------------------- THE FALLBACK COLUMN ---
       Reduced motion, no WebGL2, or a failed init: no stage, so no story to ride. The
       hero stops being the whole stage and becomes the first section of an ordinary
       scrolling page, which is what Adrian ruled and shipped in 8c975bb. overflow must
       open or the flowed beats are clipped by the old stage box. */
    .ast-scroll:not([data-live]) .ast-hero {
      min-height: 0;
      overflow: visible;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 3.5rem;
      padding: 0 var(--ast-gutter) 3.5rem;
      --ast-gutter: 1.5rem;
    }
    /* The art and its scrim are the one thing that must ignore the gutter. object-fit
       does the cropping; the box just has to be the full bleed width. */
    .ast-scroll:not([data-live]) .ast-hero :global(.hero__art),
    .ast-scroll:not([data-live]) .hero__scrim {
